# KR-05 — an exposure profile does not make its subject a stimulation.
#
# sstim-ex:hasExposureProfile carries a union domain over StimulationProtocol,
# StimulationTechnique, Stimulation and Preset. An exploratory protocol may hold
# an exposure profile without being any of them: ADR 0034 deliberately reparented
# sstim-ex:ExploratoryProtocol off SensoryStimulationProtocol so that a baseline
# defined by the *absence* of stimulation stops being typed as sensory
# stimulation by inheritance. Three committed protocols rely on that — the
# silence/darkness baseline, the smell/taste device boundary, and the wifi EM
# field hypothesis — and all three use hasExposureProfile.
#
# A union domain entails membership in the anonymous union, never in a named
# disjunct, so the reasoner infers nothing here. Narrowing the domain back to a
# single class would infer that named class instead, and a silence baseline
# would become a stimulation. This query returns rows only if that happens.
PREFIX rdf:      <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX sstim:    <https://w3id.org/sstim#>
PREFIX sstim-ex: <https://w3id.org/sstim/exposure#>

SELECT ?protocol ?unintendedType WHERE {
  ?protocol rdf:type sstim-ex:ExploratoryProtocol ;
            rdf:type ?unintendedType .
  FILTER(?unintendedType IN (
    sstim:Stimulation, sstim:SensoryStimulation,
    sstim:StimulationProtocol, sstim:StimulationTechnique, sstim:Preset
  ))
  # Seven of the ten exploratory protocols assert a sensory type outright; only
  # an *inferred* one is a defect, so anything stated in the sources is excluded.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?protocol rdf:type sstim:SensoryStimulationProtocol }
}
